 "subject": "p value in logistic regression",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2015-05-20T02:51:31Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "VQVdUyaB9eIJ",
   "from": "re...@channing.harvard.edu",
   "author": "467d202b27163786",
   "date": "2015-05-19T22:17:56Z",
   "subject": "p value in logistic regression",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div>In <font face=\"Monaco\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px\">glm.set.v1, the </span><span style=\"font-size:12px\">value</span><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px\"> is calculated via :</span></font></div><div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-size:12px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\">deviance </span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\"> obj</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\">$</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\">null.deviance </span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\">-</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\"> obj</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\">$</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\">deviance</span></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-size:12px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px\">p.value </span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px\"> </span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(4,82,24)\">1</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">-pchisq(</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px\">deviance</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">,</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px\">df</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">=</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(4,82,24)\">1</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">)</span><br></p></div><div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-size:12px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\"><br></span></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><font color=\"#061a99\" face=\"Monaco\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal\">When I calculate the p</span><span style=\"font-size:12px;line-height:normal\">value</span><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal\"> using the summary function for g</span><span style=\"font-size:12px;line-height:normal\">lm:</span></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><font color=\"#061a99\" face=\"Monaco\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal\"> </span></font>summary.glm(obj)$coefficient[&quot;Treat&quot;,&quot;Pr(&gt;|z|)&quot;]</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(6,26,153);font-family:Monaco\">the result is different.  Is there a particular reason the pvalue is calculated using the deviance and chisq distribution and is one more appropriate than the other?</span><br></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(6,26,153);font-family:Monaco\"><br></span></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(6,26,153);font-family:Monaco\">Thanks</span></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(6,26,153);font-family:Monaco\">Rose</span></p></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "DhuC2H5cTkUJ",
   "from": "Dhruva Chandramohan",
   "author": "38c56d0e82a34280",
   "date": "2015-05-20T02:29:04Z",
   "subject": "Re: [methylkit_discussion] p value in logistic regression",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I didn&#39;t write the original code, but the deviance calculation is appropriate for comparing two models- in this case, the null model which just fits using the mean over all the samples  (single parameter) vs. a model where we include a treatment effect (two parameters). The Wikipedia article on <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deviance_%28statistics%29\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deviance_%2528statistics%2529&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793233699922000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3YmCHYU3fQ1aeghq1lw8TX\">deviance</a> is short but mentions some of the relevant issues. (If we were just wanting to select a model, I believe we could just take the one with minimum <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akaike_information_criterion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akaike_information_criterion&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793233699922000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0EwumomAIKlBkzlJbMa_VR\">AIC</a>, but we need a p-value to do FDR since we have so many sites)<div><br></div><div>The top answer to <a href=\"http://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/108995/interpreting-residual-and-null-deviance-in-glm-r\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/108995/interpreting-residual-and-null-deviance-in-glm-r&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793233699923000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0fDl2RsPaqvstMOE-YpvVl\">this post</a> deals with the specific issue you noticed- see if you&#39;re observing weird things in the degrees of freedom as the original post did (the number should be higher for the null then for the residual). From what I can tell, the basic issue is that R^2 does not cleanly generalize from the OLS to the GLM setting, with the result that one is forced to choose a pseudo-R squared which shares some but not all of the properties of the standard R^2. (see explanation <a href=\"http://www.ats.ucla.edu/stat/mult_pkg/faq/general/Psuedo_RSquareds.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.ats.ucla.edu/stat/mult_pkg/faq/general/Psuedo_RSquareds.htm&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793233699923000&amp;usg=AOvVaw03UjCVdRBJNkxx_GGXJyJi\">here</a>). Hence the argument for using the deviance calculation.</div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Tue, May 19, 2015 at 6:17 PM,  <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">re...@channing.harvard.edu</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div>In <font face=\"Monaco\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px\">glm.set.v1, the </span><span style=\"font-size:12px\">value</span><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px\"> is calculated via :</span></font></div><div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-size:12px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\">deviance </span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\"> obj</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\">$</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\">null.deviance </span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\">-</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\"> obj</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\">$</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px\">deviance</span></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-size:12px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px\">p.value </span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px\"> </span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(4,82,24)\">1</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">-pchisq(</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px\">deviance</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">,</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px\">df</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">=</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(4,82,24)\">1</span><span style=\"letter-spacing:0px;color:rgb(6,26,153)\">)</span><br></p></div><div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-size:12px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:0.0px;color:#061a99\"><br></span></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><font color=\"#061a99\" face=\"Monaco\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal\">When I calculate the p</span><span style=\"font-size:12px;line-height:normal\">value</span><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal\"> using the summary function for g</span><span style=\"font-size:12px;line-height:normal\">lm:</span></font></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><font color=\"#061a99\" face=\"Monaco\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal\"> </span></font>summary.glm(obj)$coefficient[&quot;Treat&quot;,&quot;Pr(&gt;|z|)&quot;]</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(6,26,153);font-family:Monaco\">the result is different.  Is there a particular reason the pvalue is calculated using the deviance and chisq distribution and is one more appropriate than the other?</span><br></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(6,26,153);font-family:Monaco\"><br></span></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(6,26,153);font-family:Monaco\">Thanks</span></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:0px;line-height:normal;color:rgb(6,26,153);font-family:Monaco\">Rose</span></p></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
